<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Developer</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d7">Developers create code and push it to their Git repositories. Everything to be deployed is defined in the Git repository (infrastructure as code).</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">Git</data>
      <data key="d6">ThirdParty</data>
      <data key="d7">Git repositories with webhooks connected to Lagoon. Feature branches and pull requests trigger deployments.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">EKS (Lagoon)</data>
      <data key="d6">EKS</data>
      <data key="d7">Lagoon core, the open source application delivery platform, running on EKS. Listens to the repositories and decides what needs to be done (e.g. feature branch or pull request), then deploys to the hosting zones.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">EKS (Hosting Zone)</data>
      <data key="d6">EKS</data>
      <data key="d7">Hosting-zone EKS cluster running the developer environments. Operators on the cluster provision storage and databases automatically. Migrated from OpenShift to Kubernetes for more flexibility and to stay on the bleeding edge of Kubernetes.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">EC2</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Normal EC2 instances running the base infrastructure / whole base workload of the EKS cluster.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Spot</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Spot instances for development or non-production infrastructure where failure or termination of a pod can be tolerated. Cost benefit of 70-90% versus EC2. Goal is to move more workloads from EC2 to Spot.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">Aurora R6G</data>
      <data key="d6">Aurora</data>
      <data key="d7">Aurora database provisioned by an operator when a developer requests a database during deployment; exposed back to the project as a set of secrets. Uses Graviton2 R6G instances, started in non-production with no regressions seen, now moving more production workloads onto them.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">EBS GP3</data>
      <data key="d6">EBS</data>
      <data key="d7">EBS GP3 volumes for specialized workloads such as a database or a search server (OpenSearch, Solr). Adopted mid-December 2020 right after announcement, rolled out during maintenance windows; lower storage cost and better IOPS performance.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8">
      <data key="d5">EFS</data>
      <data key="d6">EFS</data>
      <data key="d7">EFS for normal file assets that do not need anything specific. Chosen transparently by the operator so developers do not need to think about storage.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Developers push code to their Git repositories.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Webhooks notify Lagoon of repository events (feature branch, pull request).</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Lagoon goes to the hosting zone and deploys everything the environment needs.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">EKS runs the base workload on normal EC2 instances.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">EKS runs development / non-production workloads on EC2 Spot instances.</data>
      <data key="d10">3'</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">When a developer needs a database, an operator provisions an Aurora database (R6G) and exposes it back to the project as a set of secrets.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">Specialized workloads (database, OpenSearch, Solr) store data on EBS GP3 volumes provisioned by the operator.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="8" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">Normal file assets are stored in EFS provisioned by the operator.</data>
      <data key="d10">0'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">amazee.io Lagoon: Open Source Application Delivery Platform on Amazon EKS</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90rWUjKjnAE</data>
    <data key="d2">amazee.io built Lagoon, an open source application delivery platform that lets developers move applications into Kubernetes without deep Kubernetes knowledge. Developers push to Git; webhooks notify Lagoon (on EKS), which deploys environments to hosting-zone EKS clusters following an infrastructure-as-code paradigm. Base workloads run on EC2, dev/non-production on EC2 Spot (70-90% cost benefit). Operators provision storage (EFS or EBS GP3) and Aurora databases (R6G Graviton2) automatically. They migrated from OpenShift to Kubernetes. Next steps: more Spot, more R6G, lower costs with savings plans.</data>
    <data key="d3">Containers, DevOps, Compute, Storage, Database</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
